<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leuven</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Louvain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leuven (French: Louvain) is a city in the Belgian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-brabant">Flemish Brabant</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dijle</a>. Its counts became dukes of Brabant in the 12th century, and the city was a centre of the medieval cloth trade. The university founded there in 1425 is the oldest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>; its library was burnt by German troops in 1914 and again in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leuven.jpg" alt="Leuven" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vandevorst, Kris · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tafelrond_Leuven_-_385956_-_onroerenderfgoed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
